    Part (a) — RcInc no-dst confirmation:
      Per Annex E §AIMS TF-N/A + Appendix A row 16:
        RcInc | TF-N/A | — | — | — | — | — | — | —
      Verified: transfer_tf_n_a("RcInc") returns NoDst marker.
      Semantics: RcInc { var } physically increments `var`'s reference
      count by 1 (writes to the RC header memory cell). The AIMS-level
      transfer is empty because (i) no new SSA value is defined, and
      (ii) the operation reads `var` for its address but does NOT
      consume `var`'s value as a TF-11-style "use" — `var` retains its
      AimsState unchanged.

    Part (b) — RcDec no-dst confirmation:
      Per Annex E §AIMS TF-N/A + Appendix A row 17:
        RcDec | TF-N/A | — | — | — | — | — | — | —
      Verified: transfer_tf_n_a("RcDec") returns NoDst marker.
      Semantics: RcDec { var } physically decrements `var`'s reference
      count by 1 (potentially triggers deallocation if count hits 0).
      Same forward-transfer rationale as RcInc: no new SSA value;
      `var`'s AimsState unchanged at AIMS layer.

    Part (c) — no backward TF-11 demand:
      Per Annex E §AIMS TF-11 table:
        RcInc/RcDec { var } | none (RC operation, not a use)
      Verified: backward_demand_tf_n_a("RcInc") = empty list;
      backward_demand_tf_n_a("RcDec") = empty list.
      Rationale: TF-11 emits backward demand on operands that REPRESENT
      A USE of a runtime value. RC operations manipulate the RC header
      directly (a memory-management side-effect, not a value-consuming
      use); demand-tracking does NOT see them as uses. Note this is
      consistent with RL-22 (RC-motion safety) — RcInc/RcDec are NOT
      RC-observable barriers FOR `v` themselves in the demand sense;
      the barrier semantics live at RL-22 + RL-26, separate from TF-11
      demand emission.

